  "description": "Sliding window variants deep dive: counting windows with at most K, exactly(K) = atMost(K) - atMost(K-1), at-least counting, the never-shrinking budget window (max consecutive ones III, fruit into baskets), minimum window substring and fixed windows with frequency maps, with steppers and Go, C++, Java and Python templates.",
  "hero": {
    "tag": "header",
    "attrs": {
      "class": "page-hero"
    },
    "html": "\r\n  <div class=\"hero-meta\"><span class=\"level-badge\" data-level=\"3\">Level 3 - Intermediate</span><span class=\"time\">45 min</span><span class=\"chip\">Deep dive</span><span class=\"chip\">Sliding window</span><span class=\"chip\">Counting</span></div>\r\n  <h1>Sliding window variants: at most K, exactly K, shortest covering</h1>\r\n  <p class=\"lead\">The primer taught you the expand / shrink / record loop. This page is about the four shapes that loop takes in real interview questions: longest window with a budget, shortest covering window, counting windows with \"at most K\", and counting with \"at least K\". Then the subtraction trick <code>exactly(K) = atMost(K) - atMost(K - 1)</code>, fixed windows with frequency maps, and the bugs interviewers watch for. Templates in Go, C++, Java and Python.</p>\r\n"
